  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "G22uG6I6tR2zudIshCTDXa3AR8Q=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:35:50.000Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "An `IProcessor` that embeds document content in-process with Transformers.js (`all-MiniLM-L6-v2` via ONNX) into PGlite's `vector` extension, answering cosine-similarity queries. No external API, no key, no server \u2014 pgvector runs inside PGlite.\n\nThe pipeline per batch:\n\n1. Collect the **last operation per document** \u2014 earlier states are superseded, so intermediate revisions are never embedded.\n2. Flatten the resulting document state into one string and truncate to the model's effective window.\n3. **Hash the text and skip re-embedding when the hash is unchanged** \u2014 embedding is by far the most expensive step, and a processor that gets re-delivered history on backfill or restart would otherwise pay for it repeatedly.\n4. Embed to a 384-dim vector and upsert into `semantic_index` (`embedding vector(384)`, HNSW index, cosine ops).\n5. Handle `DELETE_DOCUMENT` by removing the row.\n\nQueries go through `searchSimilar(text, k)`: embed the query with the same model, then `ORDER BY embedding <=> $1 LIMIT k`. With normalized embeddings, `similarity = 1 - cosine distance`.\n\n```ts\nconst pglite = new KyselyPGlite({ extensions: { vector } });  // vector ext is required\nconst db = new Kysely<SemanticSearchDB>({ dialect: pglite.dialect });\nawait up(db);\n\nconst processor = new SemanticSearchProcessor(db, embed);\nconst query = createSimilarityQuery(db, embed);\nconst results = await query.searchSimilar(\"budget allocation\", 10);\n```\n\nThree pitfalls the recipe names: the embedding dimension is **baked into the column type**, and the processor asserts `embedding.length === 384` before insert so a model swap fails loudly rather than corrupting the index; long content is **truncated, not chunked** (cut at 2,000 characters \u2014 production systems should chunk into multiple rows); and HNSW defaults (`m=16`, `ef_construction=64`) are fine until many thousands of vectors.\n\n**Directly self-relevant:** the recipe names [`@powerhousedao/knowledge-note`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@powerhousedao/knowledge-note) \u2014 the package this vault runs on \u2014 as \"**the production proof of this stack**\", and records one production-only divergence: knowledge-note **lazily `import()`s PGlite** so its processor and subgraph still register on deployments where transitive runtime deps are not installed. A Node-only recipe can use a plain import; a deployed package cannot count on that.\n\nProvenance: academy `05-Lookup/00-Cookbook.md`, Semantic Search Processor recipe (auto-generated from `github.com/powerhouse-inc/recipes/tree/main/semantic-search`), academy 6.2.2-dev.45.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-14T12:35:50.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:35:50.000Z"
    }
  },
